(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-classes-2027q-032"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-classes-2027q-032","title":"DVRUO Classes GATE CS | Computer Networks | Network layer | Weekly Quiz 5","sourceTitle":"DVRUO Classes GATE CS | Computer Networks | Network layer | Weekly Quiz 5","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO CLASSES","providerKey":"classes","series":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","seriesId":"cs-classes-2027q","num":32,"category":"other","year":"","format":"B","durationSec":2160,"info":{"orgName":"DVRUO Classes","batchName":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","subjectName":"Computer Networks"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/GO CLASSES/GO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027/051_exam_1064_GO Classes GATE CS Computer Networks Network layer Weekly Quiz 5.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>What happens when a source sends an IP packet larger than the MTU of a router along the path and the DF (Don't Fragment) bit is set?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The packet is silently dropped<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The packet is fragmented by the router<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The destination host discards the packet<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>An ICMP error message is sent back to the source</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"484849\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">A. False &nbsp;(It will be true in some cases, for example when there is an Error in Header Checksum, in that case packet will be dropped silently).<br>\n<br>\nB. False &nbsp;(Its not possible since DF is set)<br>\n<br>\nC. False &nbsp;(Router will Drop the packet bcoz DF=1 so fragments even cant reach to the Destination).<br>\n<br>\nD. True (ICMP Error message is send back to the source and again the complete Packet will be Retransmitted by the source)</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479646\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>An IP datagram of total length $1000$ bytes, including a $20$-byte IP header, needs to be transmitted over a network with a Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) of $512$ bytes. The datagram is fragmented accordingly. Fragmentation must ensure that each fragment's payload size (excluding header) is a multiple of $8$ bytes, except possibly the last fragment.<br><br>What is the Fragment Offset value (in bytes) for the third fragment?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$976$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$122$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$508$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$492$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"479645\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul><li>Payload $=980$ bytes ($1000-20$)<br>&nbsp;</li><li>First fragment carries $488$ bytes (offset $0$).<br>&nbsp;</li><li>Second fragment carries next $488$ bytes&nbsp;(offset $488$)</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Remaining $4$ bytes go in the third fragment $\\rightarrow$ offset $=488+488=976$ bytes</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Note: While the Fragment Offset field in the IP header is expressed in units of $8$ bytes (i.e., $976 / 8=122$), this question explicitly asks for the value in bytes, so the correct answer is $976$.</li></ul><p><br><img alt=\"\" width=\"1200\" height=\"967\" src=\"media/fd/fd2ae08da16c44e12354.jpg\"></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479644\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>An IP datagram arrives at a router with the following header information:<br>$$<br>\\begin{array}{ll}<br>\\text{Length of IP header:} &amp; &nbsp;\\quad 20\\ \\text{bytes} \\\\<br>\\text{Total length of IP datagram:} &amp; \\quad996\\ \\text{bytes} \\\\<br>\\text{DF flag:} &amp; \\quad 0 \\\\<br>\\text{MF flag:} &amp; \\quad 1 \\\\<br>\\text{Fragment offset:} &amp; \\quad 122 \\\\<br>\\end{array}<br>$$<br>The datagram must now be forwarded over a network with an MTU of only $500$ bytes.<br>Use the information in the IP header to infer the minimum total length of the original datagram (before it was fragmented for the first time).</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1500$ bytes<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$1972$ bytes<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1496$ bytes<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$1118$ bytes</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"479643\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul><li>&nbsp;Fragment offset $=122 \\Rightarrow$ means the payload in this fragment starts at byte $976$ of the original datagram.</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>This fragment carries $976$ bytes of data (since $996$ total - $20$ header = $976$).</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>So, the data in this fragment ends at byte $1951$.<br><br>&nbsp;</li><li>Therefore, the original datagram must have had at least $1952$ bytes of data.<br><br>&nbsp;</li><li>Adding the IP header of $\\mathbf{2 0}$ bytes from the original datagram (not from the fragments), the minimum total length of the original datagram is $\\mathbf{1972}$ bytes.</li></ul></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479642\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Match the following IP header fields (Column B) to their respective functions (Column A). Use option $\\mathbf{f)}$ if none of the given fields apply.<br>$$<br>\\begin{array}{ll}<br>\\text{Column A (Purpose)} &amp; \\text{Column B (Field)} \\\\<br>\\text{i. Used for fragmentation} &amp; \\text{a) 16-bit Identification Field} \\\\<br>\\text{ii. Indicates presence of options} &amp; \\text{b) 4-bit Header Length} \\\\<br>\\text{iii. Prevents infinite packet looping} &amp; \\text{c) 8-bit TTL} \\\\<br>\\text{iv. Detects corruption of packet payload} &amp; \\text{d) 16-bit Checksum} \\\\<br>&amp; \\text{e) 8-bit Protocol} \\\\<br>&amp; \\text{f) none-of-the-above} \\\\<br>\\end{array}<br>$$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{i} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{a}, \\mathrm{ii} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{b}, \\mathrm{iii} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{c}, \\mathrm{iv} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{d}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{i} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{a}, \\mathrm{ii} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{b}, \\mathrm{iii} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{c}, \\mathrm{iv} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{f}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>i $\\rightarrow \\mathrm{c}$, ii $\\rightarrow \\mathrm{a}$, iii $\\rightarrow \\mathrm{e}$, iv $\\rightarrow \\mathrm{d}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{i} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{e}, \\mathrm{ii} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{b}, \\mathrm{iii} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{a}, \\mathrm{iv} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{f}$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 7)</small></h4><a name=\"479639\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">$\\mathbf{i} \\rightarrow \\mathbf{a}$ : The Identification Field is used to uniquely identify fragments of an original IP datagram so they can be reassembled correctly.<br>\n<br>\n$\\mathbf{i i} \\rightarrow \\mathbf{b}$ : The Header Length field is 4 bits and indicates the length of the IP header. If it is more than 5 (i.e., more than 20 bytes), options are present.<br>\n<br>\n$\\mathbf{iii} \\rightarrow \\mathbf{c}$ : The TTL (Time to Live) field is used to prevent a datagram from circulating indefinitely. Each router decrements it by 1 , and the packet is dropped when TTL reaches 0.<br>\n<br>\n$\\mathbf{iv} \\rightarrow \\mathbf{f}$ : The Checksum field only verifies the IP header, not the payload. Therefore, none of the listed fields detect corruption of the payload.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"500344\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><h3><b>Task:</b> Match the IP header fields to their functions.</h3><p data-path-to-node=\"9\"><b>Analysis:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"10\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,0,0\"><b>i. Used for fragmentation:</b> The <b>16-bit Identification Field</b> (along with flags and offset) is used to identify which datagram a fragment belongs to. $\\rightarrow$ <b>(a)</b></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,1,0\"><b>ii. Indicates presence of options:</b> The <b>4-bit Header Length</b> (IHL) tells you how long the header is. If IHL &gt; 5, it means options are present. $\\rightarrow$ <b>(b)</b></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,2,0\"><b>iii. Prevents infinite packet looping:</b> The <b>8-bit TTL</b> (Time to Live) is decremented at each hop. If it hits 0, the packet is discarded. $\\rightarrow$ <b>(c)</b></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"10,3,0\"><b>iv. Detects corruption of packet payload:</b> The IPv4 header checksum <b>only covers the header</b>. It does <i>not</i> check the payload. The transport layer (TCP/UDP) handles payload checksums. Therefore, none of the listed IP header fields do this. $\\rightarrow$ <b>(f)</b></p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"11\"><b>Matching:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"12\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,0,0\">i $\\rightarrow$ a</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,1,0\">ii $\\rightarrow$ b</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,2,0\">iii $\\rightarrow$ c</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,3,0\">iv $\\rightarrow$ f</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"13\"><b>Answer:</b> <b>B</b></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479638\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":5,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A router receives a $1500$-byte IPv4 packet (including a $20$-byte IP header), with IP Identification field set to $\\mathbf{x}$, the MF (More Fragments) flag set to $1$, and the DF (Don't Fragment) flag set to $0$. The router needs to forward the packet over a link with an MTU of $1000$ bytes, so fragmentation is required.<br><br>Which of the following statements about the IP header fields of the second fragment are correct?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The value of the Identification field in the second fragment is $\\mathbf{x}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The Total Length field in the second fragment is $\\mathbf{5 2 0}$ bytes<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The Fragment Offset field in the second fragment is $\\mathbf{9 8 0}$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The MF (More Fragments) flag in the second fragment is $\\mathbf{1}$</p>"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"534519\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The key observation is the value of the <b>MF (More Fragments)</b> flag.</p><ul><li>IP Identification = <b>x</b></li><li>MF = <b>1</b></li><li>DF = <b>0</b></li></ul><p>Since <b>MF = 1</b>, the received packet is already a fragment of a larger IP datagram. The router must fragment this packet further because the outgoing MTU is only 1000 bytes.</p><hr><p>In IPv4, all fragments of the same original datagram carry the same Identification field.</p><div style=\"text-align:center;\">$$ \\text{Identification in second fragment} = x $$</div><p><b>Option A is Correct.</b></p><hr><p>The packet contains 1480 bytes of payload (1500 − 20).</p><p>With MTU = 1000 bytes, the first new fragment can carry 976 bytes of payload, leaving:</p><div style=\"text-align:center;\">$$ 1480 - 976 = 504 $$</div><p>Therefore, the second fragment size is:</p><div style=\"text-align:center;\">$$ 504 + 20 = 524 \\text{ bytes} $$</div><p>Hence, <b>Option B is Incorrect</b> (not 520 bytes).</p><hr><p>The Fragment Offset is measured in units of 8 bytes.</p><div style=\"text-align:center;\">$$ \\frac{976}{8} = 122 $$</div><p>Thus, the offset of the second fragment is <b>122</b>, not 980.</p><p><b>Option C is Incorrect.</b></p><hr><p>Since the original fragment already had <b>MF = 1</b>, there are more fragments of the original datagram after it. Therefore, every fragment created from it must also have:</p><div style=\"text-align:center;\">$$ MF = 1 $$</div><p><b>Option D is Correct.</b></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 10)</small></h4><a name=\"479950\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><em>Given:</em></p><ul><li><em>Total packet size = 1500 Bytes</em></li><li><em>IP Header size = 20 Bytes</em></li><li><em>MTU = 1000 Bytes</em></li></ul><div>Therefore, IP Payload size = 1500 - 20 = 1480 Bytes</div><div>Also, MTU can have max payload of 1000 - 20 = 980 Bytes.</div><div><span style=\"background-color:#999999;\">IP fragment offset must be a multiple of 8 bytes. (980 / 8 = 122.5).</span></div><div>So we choose 976B as the largest valid payload size for each fragment.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>Therefore, there will be 2 fragments.</div><div>Fragment 1:</div><ul><li>Payload = 976 Bytes</li><li>Total length = 976 + 20 = 996 Bytes</li><li>Fragment offset = 0</li><li>MF = 1</li><li>identification = x</li></ul><div><strong>Fragment 2</strong></div><ul><li>Payload = 504 Bytes</li><li><strong>Total length = 504+20 = 524 Bytes</strong></li><li>Fragment offset = 976/8 = 122&nbsp;</li><li><strong>MF = 0 (because it is the last fragment of the current packet)</strong></li><li><strong>Identification = x</strong></li></ul><div><strong>Answer : A only</strong></div></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"483061\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Answer will be AD the original packet has more packets after it so after fragmentation fragmented packets will have some packets after them B is given incorrect as total length is 524 not 520Bytes</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"499147\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>an example for option d</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"1115\" height=\"626\" src=\"media/aa/aa44239745582b7c0ed3.png\"></p><p>answers is A,D</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"480105\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">A,D</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 6 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"479773\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">The second fragment is the last fragment so the mf bit should be zero not 1 correct answr will be AB not ABD</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479637\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Host A sends a total of $2400$ bytes<strong> </strong>to Host B over a network path as shown below.<br><br>This includes a $20$-byte TCP header and a $20$-byte IP header.</p><div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"623\" height=\"108\" src=\"media/41/417b9a9ab8ee7b1e5044.jpg\"></div><p>Assume the probability that any fragment arrives intact over any link is \\( p \\), and there is no retransmission.</p><p>What is the probability that the entire $2400$ bytes arrive at Host B without the need for retransmission?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\\( p^6 \\)<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\\( p^7 \\)<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\\( p^8 \\)<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\\( p^9 \\)</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"479636\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul><li>Host A segments this data into two IP packets due to its own MTU of $1500$ bytes:</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Packet $1$: 1480 bytes data $+20$ bytes IP header $=1500$ bytes</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Packet $2$: 880 bytes data $+20$ bytes IP header $=900$ bytes</li></ul><p><br>These two packets are sent over the first link:<br><br>$$<br>\\text { Host A } \\rightarrow \\text { Router A: } 2 \\text { packet transmissions }<br>$$<br><br>Router A must fragment any packet exceeding the MTU of 1000 bytes. Packet 1 is fragmented into:<br>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Fragment $1.1$: 980 bytes data $+20$ bytes header $=1000$ bytes</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Fragment $1.2 $: 500 bytes data $+20$ bytes header $=520$ bytes</li></ul><p><br>Packet $2$ does not require fragmentation. Thus, Router A sends $3$ IP fragments on the next link:<br><br>$$<br>\\text { Router A } \\rightarrow \\text { Router B: } 3 \\text { fragment transmissions }<br>$$<br><br>Router B forwards all 3 fragments unchanged to Host B:<br><br>Router B $\\rightarrow $ Host B: $3$ fragment transmissions<br><br>$$<br>\\begin{array}{|l|c|}<br>\\hline<br>\\textbf{Link} &amp; \\textbf{Number of Transmissions} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>\\text{Host A} \\rightarrow \\text{Router A} &amp; 2 \\\\<br>\\text{Router A} \\rightarrow \\text{Router B} &amp; 3 \\\\<br>\\text{Router B} \\rightarrow \\text{Host B} &amp; 3 \\\\<br>\\hline<br>\\textbf{Total} &amp; \\mathbf{8} \\\\<br>\\hline<br>\\end{array}<br>$$<br><br>Each of the $8$ transmissions must succeed independently with probability $p$.<br>&nbsp;</p><p>Therefore, the probability that the entire message is successfully received and reassembled is $\\mathbf{p^{8}}$<br>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"538867\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><h3 data-start=\"20\" data-end=\"47\">Step 1: Original packet</h3><ul data-start=\"49\" data-end=\"137\"><li data-start=\"49\" data-end=\"83\">Total IP packet = <strong data-start=\"69\" data-end=\"83\">2400 bytes</strong></li><li data-start=\"84\" data-end=\"110\">IP header = <strong data-start=\"98\" data-end=\"110\">20 bytes</strong></li><li data-start=\"111\" data-end=\"137\">Payload = <strong data-start=\"123\" data-end=\"137\">2380 bytes</strong></li></ul><hr data-start=\"139\" data-end=\"142\"><h3 data-start=\"144\" data-end=\"175\">Step 2: Link 1 (MTU = 1500)</h3><p data-start=\"177\" data-end=\"206\">Maximum payload per fragment:</p><p>1500−20=1480&nbsp;bytes1500 - 20 = 1480 \\text{ bytes}1500−20=1480&nbsp;bytes</p><p data-start=\"246\" data-end=\"249\">So,</p><ul data-start=\"251\" data-end=\"301\"><li data-start=\"251\" data-end=\"276\">Fragment 1 = 1480 bytes</li><li data-start=\"277\" data-end=\"301\">Fragment 2 = 900 bytes</li></ul><p data-start=\"303\" data-end=\"321\">➡️ <strong data-start=\"306\" data-end=\"321\">2 fragments</strong></p><hr data-start=\"323\" data-end=\"326\"><h3 data-start=\"328\" data-end=\"359\">Step 3: Link 2 (MTU = 1000)</h3><ul data-start=\"361\" data-end=\"497\"><li data-start=\"361\" data-end=\"437\">Fragment 1 (1500 bytes) is too large, so it is split into <strong data-start=\"421\" data-end=\"436\">2 fragments</strong>.</li><li data-start=\"438\" data-end=\"497\">Fragment 2 (920 bytes) fits, so it is <strong data-start=\"478\" data-end=\"496\">not fragmented</strong>.</li></ul><p data-start=\"499\" data-end=\"525\">➡️ Total = <strong data-start=\"510\" data-end=\"525\">3 fragments</strong></p><hr data-start=\"527\" data-end=\"530\"><h3 data-start=\"532\" data-end=\"563\">Step 4: Link 3 (MTU = 1500)</h3><p data-start=\"565\" data-end=\"605\">Routers <strong data-start=\"573\" data-end=\"594\">do not reassemble</strong> fragments.</p><p data-start=\"607\" data-end=\"640\">So <strong data-start=\"610\" data-end=\"625\">3 fragments</strong> are forwarded.</p><hr data-start=\"642\" data-end=\"645\"><h3 data-start=\"647\" data-end=\"670\">Step 5: Probability</h3><p data-start=\"672\" data-end=\"721\">Each fragment must successfully cross every link.</p><table data-start=\"723\" data-end=\"844\"><thead data-start=\"723\" data-end=\"743\"><tr data-start=\"723\" data-end=\"743\"><th data-start=\"723\" data-end=\"730\" data-col-size=\"sm\">Link</th><th data-start=\"730\" data-end=\"743\" data-col-size=\"sm\">Fragments</th></tr></thead><tbody data-start=\"765\" data-end=\"844\"><tr data-start=\"765\" data-end=\"790\"><td data-start=\"765\" data-end=\"785\" data-col-size=\"sm\">Host A → Router A</td><td data-start=\"785\" data-end=\"790\" data-col-size=\"sm\">2</td></tr><tr data-start=\"791\" data-end=\"818\"><td data-start=\"791\" data-end=\"813\" data-col-size=\"sm\">Router A → Router B</td><td data-col-size=\"sm\" data-start=\"813\" data-end=\"818\">3</td></tr><tr data-start=\"819\" data-end=\"844\"><td data-start=\"819\" data-end=\"839\" data-col-size=\"sm\">Router B → Host B</td><td data-col-size=\"sm\" data-start=\"839\" data-end=\"844\">3</td></tr></tbody></table><p data-start=\"846\" data-end=\"895\">Total successful fragment transmissions required:</p><p>2+3+3=8</p><p data-start=\"918\" data-end=\"968\">Each transmission succeeds with probability <strong data-start=\"962\" data-end=\"967\">p</strong>.</p><p data-start=\"970\" data-end=\"973\">So, for 8 transmission probability will be ,</p><p>p^8​</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479635\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":7,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>During normal IP packet forwarding by a router, which of the following packet fields are updated?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>IP header Source address<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>IP header Destination address<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>IP header TTL<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>IP header checksum</p>"}],"answer":["C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"479961\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Following packet fields are changed:<br>1. IP header TTL : Each router decrements TTL by 1 to prevent forwarding loops.</p><p>2. IP header checksum : The header checksum need to be recalculated since the TTL value changed.</p><p><strong>Answer: C, D</strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479628\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"An IP router uses longest prefix matching and has the following forwarding table:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{array}{l}<br>\n&nbsp;\\\\<br>\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\text{Prefix} &amp; \\text{Interface} \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n128.2.0.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 1} \\\\<br>\n128.2.1.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 2} \\\\<br>\n128.2.2.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 2} \\\\<br>\n128.2.3.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 3} \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array} \\\\<br>\n\\\\<br>\n\\text{The router can only store $3$ prefix entries.} \\\\<br>\n\\text{Which of the following options gives a functionally equivalent forwarding table?} \\\\<br>\n\\\\<br>\n\\text{A.} \\\\<br>\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\text{Prefix} &amp; \\text{Interface} \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n128.2.0.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 1} \\\\<br>\n128.2.1.0/23 &amp; \\text{Interface 2} \\\\<br>\n128.2.3.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 3} \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array} \\\\<br>\n\\\\<br>\n\\text{B.} \\\\<br>\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\text{Prefix} &amp; \\text{Interface} \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n128.2.0.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 1} \\\\<br>\n128.2.1.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 2} \\\\<br>\n128.2.2.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 2} \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array} \\\\<br>\n\\\\<br>\n\\text{C.} \\\\<br>\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\text{Prefix} &amp; \\text{Interface} \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n128.2.0.0/22 &amp; \\text{Interface 2} \\\\<br>\n128.2.0.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 1} \\\\<br>\n128.2.3.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 3} \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array} \\\\<br>\n\\\\<br>\n\\text{D.} \\\\<br>\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\text{Prefix} &amp; \\text{Interface} \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n128.2.0.0/22 &amp; \\text{Interface 1} \\\\<br>\n128.2.1.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 2} \\\\<br>\n128.2.3.0/24 &amp; \\text{Interface 3} \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array}<br>\n\\end{array}<br>\n$$","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Option A</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Option B</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Option C</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Option D</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"479625\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">$128.2.0.0/22$ covers the range $128.2.0.0 - 128.2.3.255$, so it broadly defaults to Interface 2.<br>\n<br>\nMore specific entries like $/24$ for $128.2.0.0$ and $128.2.3.0$ override it using longest prefix match.<br>\n<br>\nThus, routing behavior remains consistent with the original $4$-entry table.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"500349\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\">Based on the principles of <b>Longest Prefix Matching</b>, here is the step-by-step solution to find the functionally equivalent forwarding table.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>Analysis of the Original Table</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"2\">The router has four contiguous Class C (/24) subnets:</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"3\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,0\"><b><code>128.2.0.0/24</code></b> $\\rightarrow$ <b>Interface 1</b></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,1,0\"><b><code>128.2.1.0/24</code></b> $\\rightarrow$ <b>Interface 2</b></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,2,0\"><b><code>128.2.2.0/24</code></b> $\\rightarrow$ <b>Interface 2</b></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,3,0\"><b><code>128.2.3.0/24</code></b> $\\rightarrow$ <b>Interface 3</b></p></li></ol><p data-path-to-node=\"4\">We need to compress this into <b>3 entries</b>. The standard way to do this is to create a \"default\" aggregate route that covers all four subnets and points to the most common interface, then add specific entries for the exceptions.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"5\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>Aggregation Strategy</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"6\"><li><p>Identify the Supernet:</p><p>The four subnets 0.0, 1.0, 2.0, and 3.0 can be aggregated into a single /22 block: 128.2.0.0/22. This block covers the range 128.2.0.0 to 128.2.3.255.</p></li><li><p>Identify the \"Majority\" Interface:</p><p>Interface 2 is the destination for two of the subnets (1.0 and 2.0). This makes it the best candidate for the aggregate route.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"6,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,1,1,0,0\"><b>Proposed Rule 1:</b> <code>128.2.0.0/22</code> $\\rightarrow$ <b>Interface 2</b></p></li></ul></li><li><p>Identify Exceptions:</p><p>Now we need to add specific rules for the subnets that should not go to Interface 2.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1,0,0\">Subnet <code>0.0</code> needs to go to Interface 1.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1,0,1,0,0\"><b>Proposed Rule 2:</b> <code>128.2.0.0/24</code> $\\rightarrow$ <b>Interface 1</b></p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1,1,0\">Subnet <code>3.0</code> needs to go to Interface 3.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"6,2,1,1,1,0,0\"><b>Proposed Rule 3:</b> <code>128.2.3.0/24</code> $\\rightarrow$ <b>Interface 3</b></p></li></ul></li></ul></li></ol><p data-path-to-node=\"7\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>Verification of Option C</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"8\">Let's test Option C against our requirements using <b>Longest Prefix Match</b>:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,0\"><b>Packet to <code>128.2.0.x</code>:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,1,0,0\">Matches <code>128.2.0.0/22</code> (Interface 2).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,1,1,0\">Matches <code>128.2.0.0/24</code> (Interface 1).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,0,1,2,0\"><b>Result:</b> Longest match is <b>/24</b>. Goes to <b>Interface 1</b>. (Correct)</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,1,0\"><b>Packet to <code>128.2.1.x</code>:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,1,1,0,0\">Matches <code>128.2.0.0/22</code> (Interface 2).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,1,1,1,0\">Does not match any specific /24 entry.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,1,1,2,0\"><b>Result:</b> Goes to <b>Interface 2</b>. (Correct)</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,2,0\"><b>Packet to <code>128.2.2.x</code>:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,2,1,0,0\">Matches <code>128.2.0.0/22</code> (Interface 2).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,2,1,1,0\">Does not match any specific /24 entry.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,2,1,2,0\"><b>Result:</b> Goes to <b>Interface 2</b>. (Correct)</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,0\"><b>Packet to <code>128.2.3.x</code>:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1,0,0\">Matches <code>128.2.0.0/22</code> (Interface 2).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1,1,0\">Matches <code>128.2.3.0/24</code> (Interface 3).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"9,3,1,2,0\"><b>Result:</b> Longest match is <b>/24</b>. Goes to <b>Interface 3</b>. (Correct)</p></li></ul></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"10\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>Why other options fail:</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"11\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,0,0\"><b>A:</b> The entry <code>128.2.1.0/23</code> is problematic. In CIDR, a /23 block starting at <code>.1</code> is invalid (it would start at <code>.0</code>). Even if interpreted loosely, this table structure doesn't correctly cover the <code>2.0</code> subnet or misdirects others.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,1,0\"><b>B:</b> This table is missing an entry for the <code>128.2.3.0</code> subnet entirely. Packets for <code>3.0</code> would be dropped.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"11,2,0\"><b>D:</b> The aggregate route points to <b>Interface 1</b>. This means <code>128.2.2.0</code> (which has no specific entry) would default to Interface 1, which is <b>incorrect</b> (it should go to Interface 2).</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"12\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>Correct Answer</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"13\"><b>Option C</b></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479624\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following topology, where a node labelled with $\\mathbf{N}$ denotes an end system, $\\mathbf{H}$ denotes a hub, $\\mathbf{S}$ denotes a switch, and $\\mathbf{R}$ denotes a router:<br><br><img alt=\"\" width=\"446\" height=\"231\" src=\"media/92/92431e5c59bcd97c6621.png\"><br><br>All links are Ethernet.<br>In an Ethernet-based network, suppose node $N 1$ constructs a packet and accidentally flips a single bit in the IP header before constructing the Ethernet frame; i.e., Ethernet CRC is calculated after the bit flip. Where in the network will this error be detected and the packet dropped?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>At the hub $H 1$, due to Ethernet CRC failure.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>At the router $R 1$, due to invalid IP header checksum.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>At the router $R 2$, due to invalid IP header checksum.<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>At the switch $S 1$, due to Ethernet CRC failure.</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"479623\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Option A:</strong> <span style=\"color:#c0392b;\"><strong>Incorrect</strong>.</span><br><br>A hub operates at Layer $1$ (physical layer) and does not inspect or validate CRC. It simply forwards electrical signals to all ports.<br><br><strong>Option B:</strong> <span style=\"color:#16a085;\"><strong>Correct.</strong></span><span style=\"color:#27ae60;\"><strong> </strong></span><br><br>The IP header has its own checksum field. Since the bit flip occurs before Ethernet CRC calculation, the Ethernet frame is valid. However, when router $R 1$ checks the IP header, the IP checksum will not match, and the router will drop the packet.<br><br><strong>Option C:</strong> <span style=\"color:#c0392b;\"><strong>Incorrect</strong>.</span><br><br>The error will be detected at router $R 1$ itself, so the packet will never reach $R 2$.<br><br><strong>Option D:</strong> <span style=\"color:#c0392b;\"><strong>Incorrect</strong>.</span><br><br>The Ethernet CRC was calculated after the bit flip, so the frame appears valid at the data link layer. Moreover, switches do not inspect IP headers; they only forward based on MAC addresses.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"533401\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><h2 data-section-id=\"y8wgl3\" data-start=\"111\" data-end=\"127\">Key Statement</h2><blockquote data-start=\"129\" data-end=\"243\"><p data-start=\"131\" data-end=\"243\">N1 constructs a packet and accidentally flips a bit in the IP header <strong data-start=\"200\" data-end=\"242\">before constructing the Ethernet frame</strong>.</p></blockquote><p data-start=\"245\" data-end=\"256\">This means:</p><ol data-start=\"258\" data-end=\"423\"><li data-section-id=\"pjxe73\" data-start=\"258\" data-end=\"282\">IP packet is created.</li><li data-section-id=\"15shg98\" data-start=\"283\" data-end=\"322\">One bit in the IP header is changed.</li><li data-section-id=\"24c1zr\" data-start=\"323\" data-end=\"357\">Ethernet frame is then created.</li><li data-section-id=\"1t5hwuu\" data-start=\"358\" data-end=\"423\">Ethernet CRC is calculated on the already-corrupted IP packet.</li></ol><p data-start=\"425\" data-end=\"476\">So the Ethernet frame is <strong data-start=\"450\" data-end=\"475\">internally consistent</strong>.</p><hr data-start=\"478\" data-end=\"481\"><h2 data-section-id=\"1nodhd6\" data-start=\"483\" data-end=\"515\">Why not Ethernet CRC failure?</h2><p data-start=\"517\" data-end=\"580\">Ethernet CRC detects errors that occur <strong data-start=\"556\" data-end=\"579\">during transmission</strong>.</p><p data-start=\"582\" data-end=\"587\">Here:</p><pre data-start=\"589\" data-end=\"713\">&nbsp;</pre><pre><code>Original IP packet\n       ↓\nBit flipped in IP header\n       ↓\nEthernet CRC calculated\n       ↓\nFrame transmitted</code></pre><p data-start=\"715\" data-end=\"803\">Since CRC was computed after the bit flip, the receiver computes the same CRC and finds:</p><pre data-start=\"805\" data-end=\"844\">&nbsp;</pre><pre><code>Received CRC = Computed CRC</code></pre><p data-start=\"846\" data-end=\"864\">No Ethernet error.</p><p data-start=\"866\" data-end=\"876\">Therefore:</p><h3 data-section-id=\"1yuneg3\" data-start=\"878\" data-end=\"919\">A. Hub H1 due to Ethernet CRC failure</h3><p data-start=\"920\" data-end=\"927\">&nbsp;<strong>Wrong</strong></p><h3 data-section-id=\"ao79sg\" data-start=\"929\" data-end=\"973\">D. Switch S1 due to Ethernet CRC failure</h3><p data-start=\"974\" data-end=\"981\">&nbsp;<strong>Wrong</strong></p><hr data-start=\"983\" data-end=\"986\"><h2 data-section-id=\"9js252\" data-start=\"988\" data-end=\"1021\">What about IP Header Checksum?</h2><p data-start=\"1023\" data-end=\"1128\">When N1 originally created the IP packet, the IP header checksum was computed for the <strong data-start=\"1109\" data-end=\"1127\">correct header</strong>.</p><p data-start=\"1130\" data-end=\"1170\">Then a bit got flipped in the IP header.</p><p data-start=\"1172\" data-end=\"1176\">Now:</p><pre data-start=\"1178\" data-end=\"1223\">&nbsp;</pre><pre><code>Header changed\nChecksum unchanged</code></pre><p data-start=\"1225\" data-end=\"1274\">Therefore the IP header checksum becomes invalid.</p><hr data-start=\"1276\" data-end=\"1279\"><h2 data-section-id=\"1m95fha\" data-start=\"1281\" data-end=\"1313\">Where is IP checksum checked?</h2><ul data-start=\"1315\" data-end=\"1461\"><li data-section-id=\"1px1v2q\" data-start=\"1315\" data-end=\"1348\">Hubs do not examine IP headers.</li><li data-section-id=\"1sojjzv\" data-start=\"1349\" data-end=\"1410\">Switches operate at Layer 2 and do not verify IP checksums.</li><li data-section-id=\"u7grww\" data-start=\"1411\" data-end=\"1461\">Routers examine the IP header before forwarding.</li></ul><p data-start=\"1463\" data-end=\"1532\">Thus the <strong data-start=\"1472\" data-end=\"1513\">first router that receives the packet</strong> detects the error.</p><p data-start=\"1534\" data-end=\"1558\">Looking at the topology:</p><pre data-start=\"1560\" data-end=\"1594\">&nbsp;</pre><pre><code>N1 → H1 → H2 → R1 → R2</code></pre><p data-start=\"1596\" data-end=\"1635\">The first router encountered is <strong data-start=\"1628\" data-end=\"1634\">R1</strong>.</p><p data-start=\"1637\" data-end=\"1710\">R1 checks the IP header checksum, finds it invalid, and drops the packet.</p><hr data-start=\"1712\" data-end=\"1715\"><h2 data-section-id=\"102aayh\" data-start=\"1717\" data-end=\"1726\"><strong>Answer</strong></h2><p data-start=\"1728\" data-end=\"1789\"><strong data-start=\"1730\" data-end=\"1789\">B. At the router R1, due to invalid IP header checksum.</strong></p><p data-start=\"2040\" data-end=\"2062\" data-is-last-node=\"\" data-is-only-node=\"\"><strong data-start=\"2040\" data-end=\"2061\">Correct option: B</strong>.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479622\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose computers $A$ and $B$ have IP addresses $10.105.1.113$ and $10.105.1.91$, respectively, and they both use the same netmask $N$. Which of the values of $N$ given below should not be used if $A$ and $B$ should belong to the same network?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$255.255 .255 .0$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$255.255 .255 .128$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$255.255 .255 .192$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$255.255.255.224$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"479964\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Goal: For each netmask, find the one that results in different network addresses for A and B.</strong></p><p>In the given options, we observe that the Netmasks only differ in the 4th octet; so we only need to expand the 4th octet to binary format and check which one results in different network addresses for A and B.</p><p>For computer A : 10.105.1.(0111 0001)</p><p>For computer B: 10.105.1.(01011011)</p><p>While checking you will notice that for option D:</p><p>255.255.255.(1110 0000) is the netmask.</p><p>The third bit from the left in the 4th octet is different for A and B. Thus option D will result in different network addresses for A and B.<br><br><strong>Answer: D</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479621\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<meta><p>An IP datagram is fragmented into three packets.<br><br>The following table shows the first 20 bytes of each IP fragment, starting from the IP header (hex values):</p><div class=\"table-container\"><table style=\"width:100%; text-align:center; font-family:monospace;\"><tbody><tr><th>📦 Packet #1</th><th>📦 Packet #2</th><th>📦 Packet #3</th></tr><tr><td>\\(\\texttt{45 00 02 54}\\)</td><td>\\(\\texttt{45 00 01 5a}\\)</td><td>\\(\\texttt{45 00 02 54}\\)</td></tr><tr><td>\\(\\texttt{42 d8 20 48}\\)</td><td>\\(\\texttt{42 d8 00 90}\\)</td><td>\\(\\texttt{42 d8 20 00}\\)</td></tr><tr><td>\\(\\texttt{40 11 fe af}\\)</td><td>\\(\\texttt{40 11 1f 62}\\)</td><td>\\(\\texttt{40 11 fe f7}\\)</td></tr><tr><td>\\(\\texttt{0a 00 01 02}\\)</td><td>\\(\\texttt{0a 00 01 02}\\)</td><td>\\(\\texttt{0a 00 01 02}\\)</td></tr><tr><td>\\(\\texttt{0a 00 01 c8}\\)</td><td>\\(\\texttt{0a 00 01 c8}\\)</td><td>\\(\\texttt{0a 00 01 c8}\\)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Based on this information, what is the correct order of the fragments for reassembling the original IP datagram?</p><div class=\"options\">","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Packet #1 → Packet #3 → Packet #2<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Packet #3 → Packet #1 → Packet #2<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Packet #2 → Packet #3 → Packet #1<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Packet #3 → Packet #2 → Packet #1</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"479641\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>In an IPv4 fragment, the <b>second row</b> (i.e., bytes 7 and 8 of the IP header) contains a <b>16-bit field</b>:</p><h3>Flags + Fragment Offset &nbsp;&nbsp; <span style=\"font-weight:normal;\">(13 bits offset, 3 bits flags)</span></h3><p>This field is provided as two hexadecimal bytes.</p><div class=\"table-container\"><table style=\"width:100%; text-align:center; font-family:monospace; border-collapse:collapse;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"padding:6px;\">Packet</th><th style=\"padding:6px;\">Flags + Offset</th><th style=\"padding:6px;\">Last 13 Bits (Hex)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"padding:6px;\">Packet #1</td><td style=\"padding:6px;\"><code>20 48</code></td><td style=\"padding:6px;\"><code>048</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px;\">Packet #2</td><td style=\"padding:6px;\"><code>00 90</code></td><td style=\"padding:6px;\"><code>090</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px;\">Packet #3</td><td style=\"padding:6px;\"><code>20 00</code></td><td style=\"padding:6px;\"><code>000</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><br>🔷 <b>Packet #1:</b> <code>20 48</code></p><ul><li>Take last 13 bits → hex = <code>048</code></li><li>Decimal = 72, so offset = \\(72 \\times 8 = \\boxed{576 \\text{ bytes}}\\)</li></ul><p>🔷 <b>Packet #2:</b> <code>00 90</code></p><ul><li>Last 13 bits = <code>090</code></li><li>Decimal = 144, so offset = \\(144 \\times 8 = \\boxed{1152 \\text{ bytes}}\\)</li></ul><p>🔷 <b>Packet #3:</b> <code>20 00</code></p><ul><li>Last 13 bits = <code>000</code></li><li>Decimal = 0, so offset = \\(0 \\times 8 = \\boxed{0 \\text{ bytes}}\\)</li></ul><p>Hence, the correct reassembly sequence is:</p><p style=\"font-size: 1.2em; text-align:center; margin-top:20px;\">\\[ \\boxed{\\text{Packet #3} \\rightarrow \\text{Packet #1} \\rightarrow \\text{Packet #2}} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\textbf{Option B} \\]</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"500343\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><section id=\"processing-state-0\"><p><b>Task:</b>&nbsp;Determine the correct order of fragments to reassemble the original IP datagram.</p></section><p data-path-to-node=\"3\"><b>Analysis:</b></p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"4\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,0\"><b>Identify Fragments:</b>&nbsp;We have Packet #1, #2, and #3. We need to look at the&nbsp;<b>Offset</b>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<b>MF (More Fragments)</b>&nbsp;flag in the IP header.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,0,0\"><b>Offset:</b>&nbsp;The 13-bit Fragment Offset is in the 2nd row, last 2 bytes (combined with Flags).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,1,0\"><b>Hex Data (Row 2):</b>&nbsp;<code>42 d8 ...</code></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,2,0\"><code>4</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<code>0100</code>&nbsp;(Flags + Offset bits). First 3 bits are flags.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,2,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,2,1,0,0\"><b>Bit 0:</b>&nbsp;Reserved (0).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,2,1,1,0\"><b>Bit 1:</b>&nbsp;DF (1).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,2,1,2,0\"><b>Bit 2:</b>&nbsp;MF (0).</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,3,0\">Wait, let's look closer at the values provided.</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,3,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,3,1,0,0\"><b>Packet #1:</b>&nbsp;Row 2 is&nbsp;<code>42 d8</code>.&nbsp;<code>4</code>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<code>0100</code>. Flag is&nbsp;<code>010</code>&nbsp;(DF=1, MF=0). Offset is&nbsp;<code>0 1101 1000</code>? No, let's re-examine the standard header layout.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,3,1,1,0\"><b>Byte 0:</b>&nbsp;Version/IHL (<code>45</code>).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,3,1,2,0\"><b>Byte 6-7:</b>&nbsp;Flags/Offset.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,3,1,3,0\"><b>Packet #1:</b>&nbsp;<code>20 48</code>.&nbsp;<code>2</code>=<code>0010</code>. Flags=<code>001</code>&nbsp;(MF=1). Offset=<code>0 0100 1000</code>&nbsp;(72×8=576).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,3,1,4,0\"><b>Packet #2:</b>&nbsp;<code>00 90</code>.&nbsp;<code>0</code>=<code>0000</code>. Flags=<code>000</code>&nbsp;(MF=0). Offset=<code>0 1001 0000</code>&nbsp;(144×8=1152).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,0,1,3,1,5,0\"><b>Packet #3:</b>&nbsp;<code>20 00</code>.&nbsp;<code>2</code>=<code>0010</code>. Flags=<code>001</code>&nbsp;(MF=1). Offset=<code>0</code>.</p></li></ul></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,1,0\"><b>Ordering:</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"4,1,1\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,1,1,0,0\"><b>First Fragment:</b>&nbsp;Must have Offset = 0.&nbsp;<b>Packet #3</b>&nbsp;fits this (<code>Offset = 0</code>).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,1,1,1,0\"><b>Middle Fragment:</b>&nbsp;Must have Offset &gt; 0 and MF = 1.&nbsp;<b>Packet #1</b>&nbsp;fits this (<code>Offset = 576</code>,&nbsp;<code>MF=1</code>).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,1,1,2,0\"><b>Last Fragment:</b>&nbsp;Must have Offset &gt; 0 and MF = 0.&nbsp;<b>Packet #2</b>&nbsp;fits this (<code>Offset = 1152</code>,&nbsp;<code>MF=0</code>).</p></li></ul></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"4,2,0\"><b>Sequence:</b>&nbsp;Packet #3&nbsp;→&nbsp;Packet #1&nbsp;→&nbsp;Packet #2.</p></li></ol><p data-path-to-node=\"5\"><b>Answer:</b>&nbsp;<b>B. Packet #3&nbsp;→&nbsp;Packet #1&nbsp;→&nbsp;Packet #</b></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479640\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":12,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A computer virus is spreading in a network of $128$ computers.</p><ul><li>At the beginning (round $0$), only $1$ computer is infected.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>In each round, every infected computer spreads the virus to $2$ new uninfected computers.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>The virus is smart and never tries to infect a computer that is already infected.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>All infections in a round happen at the same time. After that, the next round begins.</li></ul><p>How many rounds (after the starting point) are needed to infect all $128$ computers?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 13)</small></h4><a name=\"479634\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Let's count how many computers are infected at the end of each round:</p><ul><li>Round $0$: $1$ computer infected (starting point)</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Round $1$: $1$ computer infects $2$ new $\\rightarrow$ total $=1+2=3$</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Round $2$: $3$ computers infect $6$ new $\\rightarrow$ total $=3+6=9$</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Round $3$: $9$ computers infect $18$ new $\\rightarrow$ total $=9+18=27$</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Round $4$: $27$ computers infect $54$ new $\\rightarrow$ total $=27+54=81$</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Round $5$: $81$ computers infect $162$ new $\\rightarrow$ total $=81+162=243$</li></ul><p><br>After $5$ rounds (not counting the starting point), all $128$ computers are infected.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479633\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A sender is transmitting $7$ packets using the Go-Back-N protocol with a window size of $3$, an RTT of $200$ ms, and a retransmission timeout of $500$ ms. If ONLY the $5th$ packet is lost, how long does it take for all packets to be correctly received and acknowledged?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$600$ ms<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$700$ ms<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$900$ ms<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$1000$ ms</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 8)</small></h4><a name=\"499166\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c9/c95a29457435941343e5.jpg\"></p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"479632\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"574\" height=\"421\" src=\"media/08/0850f6dbadc8d81cc41d.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"531348\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Go-Back-N (Window Size = 3)<br>\n<br>\nRTT = 200 ms<br>\nTimeout = 500 ms<br>\nPacket 5 is lost<br>\n<br>\nTime →<br>\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────<br>\n<br>\nt = 0<br>\n<br>\nSender sends:<br>\n1 ───────────────►<br>\n2 ───────────────►<br>\n3 ───────────────►<br>\n<br>\n(timer running for packet 1)<br>\n<br>\nt = 200 ms<br>\n<br>\nACK1 ◄──────────────<br>\nACK2 ◄──────────────<br>\nACK3 ◄──────────────<br>\n<br>\nWindow slides<br>\n<br>\nSender sends:<br>\n4 ───────────────►<br>\n5 ─────X &nbsp;(LOST)<br>\n6 ───────────────►<br>\n<br>\n(timer for packet 5 effectively matters now)<br>\n<br>\nReceiver:<br>\n4 received<br>\n5 missing<br>\n6 discarded<br>\n<br>\nt = 400 ms<br>\n<br>\nACK4 ◄──────────────<br>\n<br>\nSender window partially moves<br>\n<br>\nSender sends:<br>\n7 ───────────────►<br>\n<br>\nReceiver still waiting for 5<br>\n7 discarded<br>\n<br>\nt = 600 ms<br>\n<br>\nDuplicate ACK4 ◄──────────────<br>\n<br>\nNo ACK5 received<br>\n<br>\nSender waits for timeout<br>\n<br>\nTimeout for packet 5:<br>\n200 + 500 = 700 ms<br>\n<br>\nt = 700 ms<br>\n<br>\nRetransmit:<br>\n5 ───────────────►<br>\n6 ───────────────►<br>\n7 ───────────────►<br>\n<br>\nt = 900 ms<br>\n<br>\nACK5 ◄──────────────<br>\nACK6 ◄──────────────<br>\nACK7 ◄──────────────<br>\n<br>\nFinal transmission sequence:<br>\n<br>\n1,2,3,4,5(lost),6,7,5,6,7<br>\n<br>\nTotal time = 900 ms</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479631\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>We can think of the network as a tree, with the video server at the root and the one million subscribers $\\left(10^{6}\\right)$ at the leaves. Assuming that the tree has degree four (i.e., each router in the tree connects to four routers closer to the leaves), then roughly how many routers does a packet pass through from the root to each subscriber?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$2$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$15$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$10$<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$20$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"479630\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Let the depth of the tree (i.e., the number of routers a packet passes through from the root to a leaf) be $h$. At each level of the tree, the number of nodes grows as:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\n\\text { Number of leaf nodes }=4^{h}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\nWe are told:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\n4^{h} \\approx 10^{6}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\nTaking logarithm base 4 on both sides:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nh=\\log _{4}\\left(10^{6}\\right)<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\nUsing the change of base formula:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nh=\\frac{\\log _{10}\\left(10^{6}\\right)}{\\log _{10}(4)}=\\frac{6}{\\log _{10}(4)}<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\nWe know:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\n\\log _{10}(4)=\\log _{10}\\left(2^{2}\\right)=2 \\cdot \\log _{10}(2) \\approx 2 \\cdot 0.3010=0.602<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\nSo:<br>\n<br>\n$$<br>\nh \\approx \\frac{6}{0.602} \\approx 9.97 \\approx 10<br>\n$$<br>\n<br>\nThus, the number of routers a packet passes through from the root to a subscriber is approximately: $10$</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"499168\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">10^6 &nbsp;~ 2^20<br>\n<br>\n(2^2)^x = 2^20<br>\n<br>\n=&gt; (2^2)^x = (2^2)^10<br>\n<br>\n=&gt; &nbsp;&nbsp;x = 10</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"524220\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"698\" height=\"213\" src=\"media/58/5862561e0160e87b2ce9.jpg\">More Accurate Answer will be&nbsp;<br><br><img alt=\"\" src=\"denied:blob:https://gateoverflow.in/09f71d8d-0cb4-446d-9de1-796474fd51d6\"></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479629\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following IP header. The checksum field is currently set to <code>0000</code>. The header is being prepared for transmission.</p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"5\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px; margin: auto;\"><tbody><tr><td align=\"center\">Version<br><b>4</b></td><td align=\"center\">IHL<br><b>5</b></td><td align=\"center\">Type of Service<br><b>00</b></td><td align=\"center\">Total Length<br><b>001C</b></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">Identification<br><b>0001</b></td><td align=\"center\">Flags<br><b>0</b></td><td align=\"center\">Fragment Offset<br><b>0000</b></td></tr><tr><td align=\"center\">Time to Live<br><b>01</b></td><td align=\"center\">Protocol<br><b>11</b></td><td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">Header Checksum<br><b>0000</b></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"4\" align=\"center\">Source Address<br><b>0A0C0E05</b></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"4\" align=\"center\">Destination Address<br><b>0C060709</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p><br>What value should be written into the checksum field?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>&nbsp;744E<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>&nbsp;8BB1<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>&nbsp;FFFF<br>&nbsp;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>&nbsp;110E</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"499153\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"888\" height=\"1280\" src=\"media/f7/f7181c09e5aa5bd94165.jpg\"></p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"479627\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The header is grouped into 2‑byte words (16 bits each).</p><p>\\[ \\begin{array}{r@{\\;}l} \\texttt{0x 45 00} \\\\ +\\ \\texttt{0x 00 1C} &amp;= \\texttt{0x 45 1C} \\\\ +\\ \\texttt{0x 00 01} &amp;= \\texttt{0x 45 1D} \\\\ +\\ \\texttt{0x 00 00} &amp;= \\texttt{0x 45 1D} \\\\ +\\ \\texttt{0x 04 11} &amp;= \\texttt{0x 49 2E} \\\\ +\\ \\texttt{0x 00 00} &amp;= \\texttt{0x 49 2E} \\\\ +\\ \\texttt{0x 0A 0C} &amp;= \\texttt{0x 53 3A} \\\\ +\\ \\texttt{0x 0E 05} &amp;= \\texttt{0x 61 3F} \\\\ +\\ \\texttt{0x 0C 06} &amp;= \\texttt{0x 6D 45} \\\\ +\\ \\texttt{0x 07 09} &amp;= \\texttt{0x 74 4E} \\\\ \\end{array} \\]</p><div class=\"rule\">&nbsp;</div><p>\\(\\text{Final sum} = \\texttt{0x 744E}\\)</p><p>\\(\\text{1's Complement of } \\texttt{0x 744E} = \\texttt{0x 8BB1}\\)</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"500348\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-path-to-node=\"0\">&nbsp;</p><h3><b>IPv4 Header Checksum Calculation</b></h3><p>&nbsp;</p><p data-path-to-node=\"1\">The correct option is most likely <b>B. 8BB1</b>, assuming a small typo in the question's values (specifically the TTL). Here is the step-by-step calculation based on the image provided.</p><p>The Algorithm:</p><p>To calculate the IPv4 header checksum:</p><ol start=\"1\" data-path-to-node=\"3\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,0,0\">Divide the header into 16-bit words.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,1,0\">Set the checksum field to <code>0000</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,2,0\">Sum all the 16-bit words using <b>one's complement arithmetic</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"3,3,0\">Take the <b>one's complement</b> (invert the bits) of the final sum.</p></li></ol><p>Step 1: Extract 16-bit Words (Hexadecimal)</p><p>Based on the image:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"5\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,0\"><b>Word 1:</b> <code>4500</code> (Version=4, IHL=5, ToS=00)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,0\"><b>Word 2:</b> <code>001C</code> (Total Length = 28 bytes)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,0\"><b>Word 3:</b> <code>0001</code> (Identification)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,3,0\"><b>Word 4:</b> <code>0000</code> (Flags=0, Fragment Offset=0)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,4,0\"><b>Word 5:</b> <code>0111</code> (TTL=<code>01</code>, Protocol=<code>11</code> hex)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,5,0\"><b>Word 6:</b> <code>0000</code> (Checksum placeholder)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,6,0\"><b>Word 7:</b> <code>0A0C</code> (Source IP: <code>0A</code>, <code>0C</code>)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,7,0\"><b>Word 8:</b> <code>0E05</code> (Source IP: <code>0E</code>, <code>05</code>)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,8,0\"><b>Word 9:</b> <code>0C06</code> (Dest IP: <code>0C</code>, <code>06</code>)</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"5,9,0\"><b>Word 10:</b> <code>0709</code> (Dest IP: <code>07</code>, <code>09</code>)</p></li></ul><p>Step 2: Calculate the Sum</p><p>We add these hexadecimal values together.</p><p>$$\\begin{array}{r@{\\quad}l} &amp; 4500 \\\\ + &amp; 001C \\\\ + &amp; 0001 \\\\ + &amp; 0000 \\\\ + &amp; 0111 \\\\ + &amp; 0A0C \\\\ + &amp; 0E05 \\\\ + &amp; 0C06 \\\\ + &amp; 0709 \\\\ \\hline \\textbf{Sum} &amp; \\mathbf{714E} \\end{array}$$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"8\"><i>(Note: The sum <code>0x714E</code> is less than <code>0xFFFF</code>, so no carry wrap-around is needed.)</i></p><p>Step 3: Calculate the Checksum</p><p>The checksum is the one's complement of the sum 714E.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>$$FFFF - 714E = \\mathbf{8EB1}$$</p><p data-path-to-node=\"11\"><b>Step 4: Analyzing the Options</b></p><ul data-path-to-node=\"12\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,0,0\"><b>Calculated Checksum:</b> <code>8EB1</code></p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"12,1,0\"><b>Option B:</b> <code>8BB1</code></p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"13\">There is a discrepancy of <code>0x0300</code> between the calculated value and Option B (<code>8EB1 - 8BB1 = 300</code>). This corresponds exactly to a difference of <b>3</b> in the <b>Time to Live (TTL)</b> field (the high byte of Word 5).</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"14\"><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,0,0\">If the <b>TTL were 4</b> (instead of 1), Word 5 would be <code>0411</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,1,0\">The Sum would increase by <code>0300</code> to become <code>744E</code>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=\"14,2,0\">The Checksum would be <code>FFFF - 744E =</code> <b><code>8BB1</code></b>.</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=\"15\">Given that <code>8BB1</code> is the only plausible option derived from the data structure, it is highly likely that the intended TTL value in the question was <b>4</b>.</p><p data-path-to-node=\"16\"><b>Answer:</b> <b>B. 8BB1</b></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/479626\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"}],"sectionsMeta":[{"name":"Technical","marks":20.0,"instructions":null}],"math":true,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":15}],"stats":{"questions":15,"maxMarks":8,"types":{"MCQ":12,"MSQ":2,"NAT":1},"withoutKey":9}};
